  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nCold front making ever so slow progress east across our area today, \nproducing area of showers with a few embedded thunderstorms as it \ndoes so. Heaviest rain remains off to our southwest in area of much \nmore robust deep layer moisture convergence and area of better mid \nlevel support. Mid level troughing driving this east moving front \ncentered across central NOAM...bookended by southwest Conus and west \nAtlantic centered ridge axes.\n\nCold front will continue to work off to the east, exiting stage \nright Thursday morning. Otherwise, large scale flow regime described \nabove remains essentially steadfast, with mid level troughing \ncontrolling our weather through the weekend. Some hints northern \nConus flow becomes a bit more zonal in flavor heading into next week \nas central NOAM troughing attempts to relax.  \n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nRain evolution through Thursday morning. Focus shifts to temperature \ntrends and addressing additional chances for wet weather for the \nremainder of the period.\n\nDetails: \n\nStill expecting at least a few rounds of showers with some embedded \nthunderstorms to rotate northeast along and ahead of the front \ntonight into Thursday morning. Focus for rain will slowly pivot \nsoutheast, likely centering near or a bit south of the Saginaw Bay \narea by later tonight and early Thursday. This will be the area most \nunder the gone for some significant rain potential as additional \nwaves, some convectively enhanced/produced, rotate up across that \nregion. Guidance derived soundings confirm this heavy rain \npotential, with saturated conditions through the entirety of the \nvertical, with some limited cape through this moisture rich \nenvironment likely supporting periods of efficient rain processes. \nDefinitely not looking at any widespread heavy rains, but wouldn't \nbe at all surprised to see some local areas receive up to and over \nan inch of rain by sunrise Thursday. Otherwise, expect a gradual \ndrying trend from northwest to southeast across our forecast \nfootprint area heading through the overnight. Threat for rain looks \nto end during Thursday morning as front and mid/upper level support \npivot off to our southeast. High pressure looks to follow, bring dry \nand seasonably mild weather to end the week.\n\nUncertainty remains significant for this weekend into early next \nweek, especially with regards to timing and placement of any \nembedded lower amplitude waves traversing much broader longwave \ntroughing. Best course of action is to continue with at least chance \nshower/storm wording Saturday through Monday. As expected, \ntemperatures are trending a bit cooler for this period, with highs \nmostly in the 70s to lower 80s. Wouldn't be surprised to see these \ntemperatures trend down even a bit further, especially if some of \nthe more cloudy and showery scenarios come to fruition. Little doubt \nexact details for this weekends/early next weeks weather will change \nin the coming day as timing and location of waves become better \nrealized.",
